  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-IP24-RAS127</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="FR">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="FR">Social dialogue and labour relations law, processes and institutions</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="FR">Social dialogue is a vital component of decent work as well as a critical means to promote the transition to formality. Mechanisms for social dialogue are however significantly underdeveloped and underutilized in the informal economy.  In addition, addressing labour relations challenges through social dialogue is one of the starting points for creating conditions for decent work in the ASEAN.

The ILO has previously provided technical assistance by developing a training toolkit aimed to enhance the capacity of social partners to engage in social dialogue and organize more effectively within the informal economy, promoting more inclusive and representative social dialogue and potentially leading to policy outcomes that better promote social justice.

For the current biennium, ILO is piloting the training toolkit before its wider application in South Asia. The proposed activities include capacity building on the toolkit, which address the actors, institutions, and modes of representation of workers and employers in the informal economy, the importance of social dialogue, and strategies to mobilize and organize informal workers and employers. Additionally, the ILO supports the development of action plans aimed at organizing more effectively in the informal economy as a basis for more inclusive social dialogue.

ILO also supports constituents social dialogue to create conditions where growth, productivity and decent work (for women and men) can go hand in hand through capacity building and social dialogue initiatives in tripartite dialogue, bi-partite dialogue between employers and workers organization(s), and strengthening dispute prevention/resolution systems.

The primary beneficiaries of the ILO&apos;s intervention are the social partners, including workers and employers in the informal economy. Key stakeholders include institutions and actors involved in social dialogue and labour relations.</narrative>
  </description>
